J1 verdict: delivered inflow Mach 14.4–15.6.  The rebound toy's tested
   envelope reaches Mach 3 — the delivery EXCEEDS the tested range by
   ~5×. The toy's mechanism (repulsive-interaction rebound) has no
   known velocity ceiling, but the honest statement is: the verified
   response covers the low end of the delivered window; the high end
   needs the toy extended. NOT covered ≠ contradicted — a gap, named.

J2 verdict: rebound duration ~1 t_heal vs required hold
   0.0427·δ t_heal ⟹ causally self-consistent for door contrasts
   δ ≲ 23. Smooth-door contrasts (order unity to tens) fit inside;
   collapsed-core contrasts are the separate absorbing-boundary ledger.

J3 verdict: transverse axes are 400–3102× larger than the fast axis at
   crossing — the compression is planar to 2.5–3.5 orders of magnitude.
   The quasi-1D geometry of the rebound toy is justified at the door.

OPEN, NAMED (the joints do not cover):
   * transverse dynamics during the rebound (the toy holds them static);
   * the wall between a rebounding pocket and a still-contracting
     metric-on exterior (M4's boundary problem, unresolved);
   * whether one pocket's outflow re-steepens its neighbors (cascade
     sequencing — unwritten);
   * the Standard-Model sector's crossing (task #14).

GRADE: the handoff is now three computed joints + four named opens.
   Consistent where tested; one delivery-envelope gap to close in the
   toy. Nothing promoted.
